{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '35,90p;145,175p;319,360p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '35,90p;145,175p;319,360p'\"","aggregated_output":"    35\t### A. Content addressing — `jing_test`\n    36\t\n    37\t- A1 [D] The address is derived from the payload alone:\n    38\t  `(segment-key x)` is `:segment/sha256-<64 lowercase hex>` and nothing else\n    39\t  enters it — not the source stream, not arrival order, not pool position.\n    40\t- A2 [D] Equal values address equally on every host. `content-hash` is\n    41\t  order-insensitive over maps and sets and total over every value, `nil`\n    42\t  included. Pinned by SHA-256 known-answer vectors on all three hosts\n    43\t  (`jing_test.cljc:56-77`), the only check that cljd's hand-rolled digest\n    44\t  mints the same addresses as the JVM and Node.\n    45\t- A3 [T→D] Distinct values address distinctly, both across types (`42` and\n    46\t  `\"42\"` do not collide) and within one type (`{:a 1}` and `{:a 2}` do not).\n    47\t  The design's \"equal supported values produce the same bytes\" leaves the\n    48\t  converse implicit; it belongs in *Canonical encoding*.\n    49\t- A4 [T→D] Minted addresses are readable EDN: the name never starts with a\n    50\t  digit, and a key survives `pr-str` → `read-string`. Addresses cross every\n    51\t  EDN boundary in the system; the `sha256-` prefix is load-bearing for that.\n    52\t- A5 [D] `segment-address?` is the strict test, and `segment-hash` throws on\n    53\t  anything that is not a valid address — foreign namespace, wrong length,\n    54\t  non-hex, uppercase.\n    55\t- A6 [D] The encoder is transitional (order-normalized `pr-str`), and\n    56\t  `content-hash`, `segment-key` and every address change together when the\n    57\t  canonical byte encoding lands. Unchanged, still open.\n    58\t\n    59\t### B. Materialization — `jing_test`, `mem_test`\n    60\t\n    61\t- B1 [D] `materialize!` derives the address itself, calls\n    62\t  `(put-content-fn address payload)`, and returns the address only after the\n    63\t  backend reports success.\n    64\t- B2 [D] A backend answers exactly `:inserted` or `:present`; any other\n    65\t  answer — `true`, `nil`, `:ok`, `\"inserted\"`, a map — is an invalid backend\n    66\t  result and throws.\n    67\t- B3 [D] On `:present` the stored value is read back and compared. An absent\n    68\t  read-back or an unequal value is an integrity failure that throws; nothing\n    69\t  is ever overwritten. Re-materializing an equal value is a no-op that\n    70\t  returns the same address.\n    71\t- B4 [D] What is stored is exactly the payload. No provenance, no source\n    72\t  identity, no stamp. Equal payloads arriving through different pool members\n    73\t  land on exactly one entry.\n    74\t- B5 [T→D] `nil` is a legal payload distinct from absence: `get` takes a\n    75\t  caller-supplied not-found and returns stored `nil` as `nil`.\n    76\t- B6 [T→D] A backend validates before it writes: the address must be a\n    77\t  segment address and must hash to the payload, else it throws and stores\n    78\t  nothing. This is the integrity precondition every backend, including a\n    79\t  future remote server, enforces at its own door.\n    80\t- B7 [T✗] `:dao.jing/content-missing` is a legal payload. This test exists\n    81\t  because an earlier sentinel was that keyword. The general rule — any value\n    82\t  is a payload, and the not-found sentinel is an opaque per-host object no\n    83\t  payload can equal — is B5; the specific keyword case is dropped. It is\n    84\t  dropped as a *case*, not as coverage: B5's executable test must include\n    85\t  keyword payloads, the value formerly used as a sentinel among them, so a\n    86\t  regression to a keyword sentinel is caught by a test rather than by prose.\n    87\t\n    88\t### C. Reads — `jing_test`\n    89\t\n    90\t- C1 [D] `get` accepts only segment addresses; arbitrary keys and mutable\n   145\t  the backend succeeds. This is not a rule the pool remembers. The core step\n   146\t  runs the effect between the read and the advance and advances only on the\n   147\t  effect's success; a throwing effect propagates before any advance exists.\n   148\t  `forward-step` has had this shape since it was written — `recur\n   149\t  next-cursor` appears only in the write-`ok` branch (`forward.cljc:130`) —\n   150\t  and the VM has it from `run-on-stream`'s publish ordering\n   151\t  (`stream_observer.cljc:141-143`). One place, three callers.\n   152\t- E6 [D] [pool] Round-robin fairness: `observe-step!` starts at `:next`, walks\n   153\t  the pool once, processes at most one payload, and yields that member's\n   154\t  turn. A continuously ready member cannot starve another; blocked and ended\n   155\t  members never prevent later members from being checked.\n   156\t- E7 [T✗] Two equal-length ready members drain in strict `A B A B` order. One\n   157\t  schedule that satisfies E6; the design says scheduling never affects the\n   158\t  content set. The test may keep it as an illustration; it is not owed.\n   159\t- E8 [D] [pool] Empty pool → `blocked`; a pool whose every non-ended member\n   160\t  answers `blocked` → `blocked`. All members ended → `end`. A closed member\n   161\t  still yields its retained payloads, then `end`.\n   162\t- E9 [D] [both] `gap` and the three defect outcomes are reported immediately\n   163\t  with `:member i` — `gap` with its recovery cursor, the defects with the\n   164\t  raw outcome map under `:result` — leave the member's cursor unchanged,\n   165\t  move the scheduling index past the member, and are reported again on that\n   166\t  member's next turn. Nothing is auto-resynchronized. The step supplies the\n   167\t  raw read and the recovery cursor as data and moves nothing; the pool\n   168\t  decides to report and not adopt.\n   169\t- E10 [D] [both] The observer never interprets a payload. The step hands the\n   170\t  value to the effect untouched; the pool hashes and stores.\n   171\t- E11 [T] [composition] What the `dao.space` drains pin, and nothing more:\n   172\t  drain until `blocked` or `end`; a `gap` is fatal to that test composition.\n   173\t- E12 [T✗] [pool] A reader answering a malformed map — missing `:ok` or\n   174\t  `:cursor` — makes `observe-step!` **throw**, rather than content-address\n   175\t  `nil` or install a nil cursor. The property it protects survives; the throw\n   319\t\n   320\t**What is not shared**, as in revision 8: attachment (the anchor is the\n   321\tcomposition's policy; the VM keeps `attach`, DaoJing has none); state (the\n   322\tcore has none, so `:ingress-gaps` never enters the seam); coordination\n   323\t(`forward`'s budget loop, the VM's `run-on-stream`, DaoJing's round-robin\n   324\tpool — three different loops over one step).\n   325\t\n   326\t**Layering.** `dao.stream.observe` sits where `forward` sits: an\n   327\tinterpreter over the contract, owned by neither storage nor the VM, requiring\n   328\tonly `dao.stream`. `forward` keeps its namespace as the thin layer that\n   329\tadds the loop, the resume allowance and the naming, because that is what it\n   330\tis now and what its consumer imports.\n   331\t\n   332\t### Decision 1 — DaoJing's observer, built on the step\n   333\t\n   334\tDaoJing's observer is pool coordination over `observe/step` with\n   335\t`materialize!` as the effect. Entry shape `{:stream <v2 reader handle>\n   336\t:cursor <opaque> :status s}`; `observer-state` validates E3 and holds\n   337\t`{:members [...] :next i}`; `adopt-cursor` is the one other cursor mutation.\n   338\tOne call of `observe-step!` walks the pool once from `:next`:\n   339\t\n   340\t```clojure\n   341\t;; per member, in turn:\n   342\t(observe/step stream cursor\n   343\t              (fn [payload] {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   344\t                             :address (jing/materialize! handle payload)}))\n   345\t;; :advance → member cursor := successor, status :dao.stream/ok, :next := after this member;\n   346\t;;            return {:signal :dao.stream/ok :address (get-in r [:effect :address])}\n   347\t;; :retry   → status :dao.stream/blocked, continue the walk\n   348\t;; :ended   → status :dao.stream/end, continue the walk\n   349\t;; :gap     → status recorded, :next := after this member;\n   350\t;;            return {:signal :dao.stream/gap :member i :cursor (:recovery r)}\n   351\t;; :defect  → status recorded, :next := after this member;\n   352\t;;            return {:signal (:outcome r) :member i :result (:read r)}\n   353\t;; :failed  → impossible: the effect answers ok or throws (E5)\n   354\t;; walk exhausted → {:signal :dao.stream/end} if every member ended, else {:signal :dao.stream/blocked}\n   355\t```\n   356\t\n   357\tSignal table:\n   358\t\n   359\t| signal                                                    | when                                                       | extra keys                                      | member cursor            |\n   360\t| --------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
