{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '165,185p;350,385p'\"","aggregated_output":"   165\t  move the scheduling index past the member, and are reported again on that\n   166\t  member's next turn. Nothing is auto-resynchronized. The step supplies the\n   167\t  raw read and the recovery cursor as data and moves nothing; the pool\n   168\t  decides to report and not adopt.\n   169\t- E10 [D] [both] The observer never interprets a payload. The step hands the\n   170\t  value to the effect untouched; the pool hashes and stores.\n   171\t- E11 [T] [composition] What the `dao.space` drains pin, and nothing more:\n   172\t  drain until `blocked` or `end`; a `gap` is fatal to that test composition.\n   173\t- E12 [T✗] [pool] A reader answering a malformed map — missing `:ok` or\n   174\t  `:cursor` — makes `observe-step!` **throw**, rather than content-address\n   175\t  `nil` or install a nil cursor. The property it protects survives; the throw\n   176\t  does not. E4 classifies a malformed answer as a `transport-error` defect and\n   177\t  reports it as data, so nothing is content-addressed and no cursor moves,\n   178\t  which is what the old test was defending. Recorded because the behaviour\n   179\t  change is deliberate: a caller that relied on the exception gets an outcome\n   180\t  map instead.\n   181\t\n   182\t### F. The file backend — `file_test`\n   183\t\n   184\t- F1 [D] Each record is `[address payload]` appended to a file; a put is\n   185\t  acknowledged `:inserted` only after the write is flushed.\n   350\t;;            return {:signal :dao.stream/gap :member i :cursor (:recovery r)}\n   351\t;; :defect  → status recorded, :next := after this member;\n   352\t;;            return {:signal (:outcome r) :member i :result (:read r)}\n   353\t;; :failed  → impossible: the effect answers ok or throws (E5)\n   354\t;; walk exhausted → {:signal :dao.stream/end} if every member ended, else {:signal :dao.stream/blocked}\n   355\t```\n   356\t\n   357\tSignal table:\n   358\t\n   359\t| signal                                                    | when                                                       | extra keys                                      | member cursor            |\n   360\t| --------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n   361\t| `:dao.stream/ok`                                          | a payload was materialized                                 | `:address`                                      | successor, from the step |\n   362\t| `:dao.stream/blocked`                                     | empty pool, or every non-ended member blocked              | —                                               | unchanged                |\n   363\t| `:dao.stream/end`                                         | every member has ended                                     | —                                               | unchanged                |\n   364\t| `:dao.stream/gap`                                         | member `i`'s position was evicted                          | `:member i`, `:cursor` (the step's `:recovery`) | unchanged                |\n   365\t| `/cursor-mismatch`, `/invalid-cursor`, `/transport-error` | member `i`'s read failed, or answered outside the contract | `:member i`, `:result` (the step's `:read`)     | unchanged                |\n   366\t\n   367\tWhat the pool adds to the step is exactly E1, E3, E6, E8 and the reporting\n   368\thalf of E9; what it inherits is E2's successor discipline, E4's totality, E5\n   369\tstructurally, E9's raw read and recovery cursor as data, and E10.\n   370\t\n   371\t### Decision 2 — The durable log is not a stream\n   372\t\n   373\t`dao.jing.file` writes its own framed file. It does not use a stream\n   374\ttransport, and none is built for it.\n   375\t\n   376\t**Why.** The file has one reader — the backend itself, once, at open — and\n   377\tone writer — the backend's own put. No cursor is ever handed out, no\n   378\tdescriptor projected, nothing attaches, and the file never sits in a pool.\n   379\tEvery property the contract exists to guarantee is unused, and a transport\n   380\twith one consumer would owe the conformance suite for nobody. The parallel is\n   381\t`dao.jing.mem`: a private atom behind the backend effects; `dao.jing.md`\n   382\talready says \"backend effects are explicit functions, not a protocol or\n   383\thidden state.\" Wrapping the file as a public `:dao.stream/type` also made\n   384\tDaoJing's storage format a transport anyone could open.\n   385\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
